   "note": "The early Pallavas of the Sanskrit charters ruled from Kanchipuram: Samudragupta's Allahabad inscription names Vishnugopa of Kanchi among the southern kings he defeated and reinstated (c. 350), and a Jain text copied at Patalika (Cuddalore) is dated in year 22 of Simhavarman, 'Lord of Kanchi' (458 CE). Cliopatria draws the Pallavas in these years only inland in southern Andhra (Cuddapah–Chittoor), so this region adds Tondaimandalam (the Palar, Kosasthalaiyar and Arani basins, from Pulicat Lake to north of the Ponnaiyar) and the Nellore coast up to the Gundlakamma; Schwartzberg's plate for c. 300–550 places the Pallavas along this coast from the Krishna down to Tondaimandalam. Simhavarman II (from 436) is said to have recovered lands up to the mouth of the Krishna lost to the Vishnukundins, but the atlas already draws the Vishnukundins there, so the coast is not drawn north of the Gundlakamma. South of about 12.6°N the region stops where Cliopatria's Kalabhra polygon begins, although Upinder Singh notes that the Kalabhras were not in power near the Penner and Vellar in the 4th century and Wikipedia's claim that the Cholas or Kalabhras held Kanchi itself in the 4th–6th centuries is uncited; the southern border is therefore uncertain. Drawn from a hand-traced extent, then cut back wherever the atlas already draws another state in the same years (Cliopatria realms and the other reconstruction layers), so it joins Cliopatria's own Pallava shape without overlapping it.",
