   ],
   "note": "Cliopatria's Pallava realm ends in 805, but the Pallavas ruled at Kanchipuram until c. 897. Under Dantivarman (c. 795–846) Pandya encroachment from the south reduced the kingdom to the country in and around Kanchipuram, and the Rashtrakuta Govinda III entered Kanchi in 803 (Rashtrakuta overlordship was brief). No Dantivarman inscriptions are known for c. 818–845, when the Telugu Choda Srikantha occupied Tondaimandalam and installed a junior Pallava, Abhimanasiddhi; Dantivarman and his son Nandivarman III recovered it about 845. The region is drawn for the whole period as the Pallava core round Kanchi, Mamallapuram and Uttaramerur (south to about the Tellaru–Marakkanam line, since the Pandyas later fought at Tellaru near Vandavasi); the interregnum is not shown separately. The north (Kalahasti, Tirupati, Nellore) is under Cliopatria's Rashtrakuta polygon and is not drawn, although Schwartzberg's plate for c. 700–975 labels the Pallavas up to Nellore and marks Pallava inscriptions at Kalahasti. Drawn from a hand-traced extent, then cut back wherever the atlas already draws another state in the same years (Cliopatria realms and the other reconstruction layers), so it joins Cliopatria's own Pallava shape without overlapping it.",
